  "worked_solution": "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{20-5}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (1+4+1+3+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 13\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =39\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
